n    uint256 cumulativePrice = cumulativePriceLast + (tradePrice * elapsedTime);\n    uint256 timeWeightedAverage = (cumulativePrice - cumulativePriceLast) / elapsedTime;\n    // Simplifies to: (cumulativePriceLast + tradePrice * elapsedTime - cumulativePriceLast) / elapsedTime\n    //              = tradePrice * elapsedTime / elapsedTime\n    //              = tradePrice  <-- ALWAYS equals spot price!\n\n    uint256 derivation = (tradePrice > timeWeightedAverage)\n        ? ((tradePrice - timeWeightedAverage) * 1e16) / timeWeightedAverage\n        : ((timeWeightedAverage - tradePrice) * 1e16) / timeWeightedAverage;\n    // derivation = |tradePrice - tradePrice| / tradePrice = 0  <-- ALWAYS zero!\n\n    return derivation <= slippage;  // 0 <= slippage -> ALWAYS true!\n}\n```\n\n**V2 Swap with Zero Protection — BuyBackBurnerUniswap.sol lines 81-95:**\n\n```solidity\nfunction _performSwap(address secondToken, uint256 secondTokenAmount, address)\n    internal virtual override returns (uint256 olasAmount)\n{\n    IERC20(secondToken).approve(router, secondTokenAmount);\n    address[] memory path = new address[](2);\n    path[0] = secondToken;\n    path[1] = olas;\n\n    uint256[] memory amounts = IUniswap(router).swapExactTokensForTokens(\n        secondTokenAmount,\n        0,              // amountOutMin = 0, NO minimum output protection!\n        path,\n        address(this),\n        block.timestamp\n    );\n    olasAmount = amounts[1];\n}\n```\n\n## Mathematical Proof\n\nLet:\n- `C` = `cumulativePriceLast` (cumulative price from the Uniswap V2 pair)\n- `P` = `tradePrice` (current spot price, i.e., `getPrice()` return value)\n- `t` = `elapsedTime` (time elapsed since last update)\n\n```\ncumulativePrice = C + P * t\ntimeWeightedAverage = (cumulativePrice - C) / t\n                    = (C + P * t - C) / t\n                    = P * t / t\n                    = P\n```\n\nTherefore `timeWeightedAverage` is identically equal to `tradePrice` (mathematical identity), and `derivation` is identically `0`.\n\n**`validatePrice()` returns true for ANY manipulated spot price.**\n\n## Attack Steps\n\nBased on the BuyBack & Burn flow described in `process.md`:\n\n**Precondition**: The BuyBackBurner contract holds secondTokens (e.g., ETH/WETH) waiting to be bought back into OLAS and burned.\n\n**Sandwich Attack:**\n\n1. **Block N-1**: The attacker buys a large amount of OLAS in the Uniswap V2 OLAS/secondToken pool, pushing the OLAS price up.\n2. **Block N**: The attacker calls `BuyBackBurner.buyBack(secondToken, amount)`:\n   - Inside `_buyOLAS`:\n     - `validatePrice(maxSlippage)` -> **always returns true** (due to the identity bug)\n     - `previousPrice = getPrice()` -> reads the **manipulated high price**\n     - `_performSwap(...)` -> swaps at `amountOutMin = 0` at the inflated price -> BuyBackBurner receives **far fewer OLAS**\n     - `tradePrice = getPrice()` -> post-swap price\n     - After-swap check: `tradePrice` is within `previousPrice +/- maxSlippage%` -> **passes** (because `previousPrice` itself is the manipulated price)\n   - A small amount of OLAS is transferred to bridge2Burner\n3. **Block N**: The attacker sells OLAS back into the Uniswap V2 pool, restoring the price and pocketing the profit.\n\n**Attacker profit** = price manipulation spread x BuyBackBurner swap amount / pool liquidity\n\n## Impact\n\n- **Direct protocol fund loss**: The BuyBackBurner receives far fewer OLAS than the fair market price during V2 buybacks. The difference is extracted by the attacker.\n- **Reduced OLAS burn**: Fewer OLAS are burned than intended, undermining the deflationary mechanism.\n- **Repeatable exploit**: `buyBack` is permissionless (anyone can call it), so the attacker can choose the optimal timing (e.g., when the BuyBackBurner has accumulated a large secondToken balance) to maximize profit.\n- **Scope**: Affects all on-chain buyback operations using the `BuyBackBurnerUniswap` + `UniswapPriceOracle` combination.\n\n## Recommended Fi